SUMMARY:Other Events: Diego Daruich\, University of Southern California -
  The Macroeconomics of Intergenerational Mental Health Dynamics
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Diego Daruich\, University of Southern California\nT
 itle: "The Macroeconomics of Intergenerational Mental Health Dynamics"\n
 \nWe develop a quantitative macroeconomic theory of child mental health.
  The theory is grounded in child psychiatry\, formalized in a life-cycle
  general equilibrium model with heterogeneous agents and endogenous chil
 d development\, and disciplined using micro data on the mental health of
  children and parents. Mental illness reduces the time parents have avai
 lable because of rumination. It also distorts their expectations through
  negative thinking and directly lowers the productivity of their investm
 ents in children. Mental health is transmitted in both directions: paren
 tal illness lowers child mental health and skills\, and child illness wo
 rsens parental mental health. Both directions are costly. Curing a child
 's mental illness is worth \\$164\,000 to the parent\, and curing a pare
 nt's mental illness is worth \\$86\,000 to the child. In general equilib
 rium with a balanced government budget\, subsidizing treatment for child
 ren is the most effective policy for child mental health\, but it reduce
 s welfare once its cost is financed. Subsidizing treatment for parents i
 ncreases welfare but does little for child mental health. Finally\, we u
 se the model to quantify the effects of removing social media on mental 
 health and welfare.\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/macrolaef-s
 eminar-diego-daruich-university-southern-california
